Exercise 5.9 asks that commuting approximable endomaps f, g : D → D have a least common fixed point x = f(x) = g(x), that f(⊥) = g(⊥) then forces fix(f) = fix(g), that in particular fix(f) = fix(f²), and what survives under the weaker hypothesis f ∘ g = g² ∘ f, with a hint pointing to Exercise 4.20. `commuting_least_common_fixed` takes a = fix(f), uses commutation to see that g(a) is a fixed point of f and hence a ⊑ g(a), and takes x to be the least fixed point of g above a; each iterate gⁿ(a) is fixed by f, so passing to the directed supremum gives f(x) = x, and every common fixed point lies above x. `fixElement_eq_of_commuting_bot` shows g(fⁿ(⊥)) = fⁿ⁺¹(⊥) by induction from the agreement at ⊥ and commutation, so g fixes fix(f), and the symmetric argument plus the least-fixed-point bound gives equality. `fixElement_iterTwice` shows fix(f) = fix(f ∘ f) because fⁿ(⊥) ⊑ f²ⁿ(⊥) = (f ∘ f)ⁿ(⊥) ⊑ fix(f²), while fix(f) is a fixed point of f ∘ f. `example_509` is the conjunction of these three. Nothing is proved from f ∘ g = g² ∘ f alone. The construction uses `fixAbove` from Exercise 4.7; Exercise 4.20 is the rolling rule fix(f ∘ g) = f(fix(g ∘ f)), which is not invoked. **Theorem:** partial — the least common fixed point, the equality of least fixed points when the maps agree at bottom, and fix(f) = fix(f²) are proved, and the question under only f ∘ g = g² ∘ f is not. **Proof:** partial — the three arguments are iteration and continuity proofs of the right claims, by a route other than the hinted Exercise 4.20, and the last question has no argument.
